import { PracticalCollectionCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> PracticalCollectionCopy = PracticalCollectionCopy {
  summary: "find returns the first matching array element as Maybe. A match returns Just; no match or an empty array returns Nothing.",
  taskTitle: "Read just the first urgent task",
  purpose: "Search from the start of a task list and display one urgent task. Review and Ship are both urgent, but the task needed here is Review, which appears first. Display not found when no task matches.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript tasks.find(task => task.urgent) also returns the first match and fits this task directly. describe explicitly checks whether the result is undefined. The example compares an urgent-task match, a nonexistent title, and an empty array.",
  benefit: "Seseragi’s items.find urgent tasks returns Maybe<Task>. Extract the task from Just to use its title, or choose a different display for Nothing. There is no need to collect every match before reading the first one, and absence is represented in the result type.",
  setup: "items and json are import aliases for std/array and std/json. struct Task defines a title string and an urgent Bool. fn urgent task: Task -> Bool = task.urgent takes one task and returns whether it is urgent.",
  after: [
    "items.find urgent tasks passes the condition function urgent first, then the array tasks. Read returns False and Review returns True, so Review is the first match and goes inside Just. The later Ship is not the returned task.",
    "describe accepts Maybe<Task>. In its match, Just task extracts the Task and returns its title, while Nothing returns not found. It does not try to read title from the Maybe<Task> value itself.",
    "\\task -> task.title == \"Missing\" is a function written at the call site. It takes one task and returns Bool by comparing the title with Missing. No task meets that condition, so the second line is not found.",
    "The third call uses the empty array items.empty<Task> (), so it also displays not found. The final [task.title | task <- tasks] is an array comprehension listing the original titles in order. They remain Read, Review, and Ship.",
  ],
  details: [
    "find checks in array order and returns the first match. Use filter when you need all matching tasks. With the multiple matches in this example, find returns Review; filter would retain Review and Ship.",
    "A matching value equal to 0, False, or an empty string still returns Just. Nothing means no element met the condition, not that a found value looks false. The displayed task example searches Task values.",
    "The condition takes a Task and returns Bool. An empty array has no elements to check, so it never calls the condition function. The input array is not changed.",
  ],
}
